            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of image quality of coronary CT angiography between 16 and 64 slices MDCT.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>364-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the image quality and visibility of coronary arteries that were performed by 16 slices and 64 slices multidetector row CT (MDCT).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Descriptive analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-eight patients suspected of hearing coronary artery disease had noninvasive coronary CT angiography performed by 16 slices MDCT and 64 slices MDCT Data were retrospectively analyzed and reviewed by two radiologists. Image quality was assessed by using a grading scale from excellent (4) to non-assessable (0) and the rate of displayed coronary branches was calculated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Four hundred twenty coronary CT angiography segments in 28 patients were evaluated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Coronary CT angiography using 64 slices multi-detector row CT provides a significantly higher image quality of coronary arteries and their branches compared with 16 slices multi-detector row CT.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
